## c2-pow | Power series | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-tests
explain 3 | A power series is a polynomial that never ends
- $ Σ cₙ(x − a)ⁿ = c₀ + c₁(x − a) + c₂(x − a)² + …
- It converges inside a *radius of convergence* R around a, and diverges outside.
- At the two endpoints, anything can happen: check them separately.
? What's the radius of convergence of a power series? :: The R such that it converges for |x − a| < R and diverges for |x − a| > R
> The geometric series is the mother of them all

quiz 3 | 1/(1 − x) = 1 + x + x² + x³ + … for which x?
* −1 < x < 1
o All x
o x < 1
o −1 ≤ x ≤ 1
= Geometric series with ratio x. Converges only for |x| < 1.
> Differentiate and integrate it to get new series for free

quiz 4 | Integrate 1/(1 + x) = 1 − x + x² − … term by term. What series do you get?
* ln(1 + x) = x − x²/2 + x³/3 − …
o eˣ
o arctan x = x − x³/3 + …
o 1/(1 + x)²
= ∫ 1/(1 + x) dx = ln(1 + x). Term by term gives the alternating series.
= At x = 1: ln 2 = 1 − 1/2 + 1/3 − …
> The radius of convergence can be set by trouble you can't see

story 5 | Why does 1/(1 + x²) stop converging at x = 1?
- Its series 1 − x² + x⁴ − … has radius 1, but the function is perfectly smooth on all real numbers.
- The trouble is at x = ±i, where 1 + x² = 0.
- *Power series feel the complex plane.* The radius is the distance to the nearest complex singularity.
> Complex analysis explains real calculus

quiz 3 | Radius of convergence of Σ xⁿ/n! ?
* Infinite
o 1
o 0
o e
= Ratio test: |x|/(n + 1) → 0 for every x.
= It's the series for eˣ, which converges everywhere.
> Next: building Taylor series by hand

## c2-tay | Taylor series workout | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-pow
explain 3 | Taylor's formula
- $ f(x) = Σ f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)(x − a)ⁿ / n!
- Each coefficient matches one more derivative at the point a.
- At a = 0 it's called a Maclaurin series.
? General term of a Taylor series about a? :: f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)(x − a)ⁿ/n!
> Five series worth knowing by heart

explain 2 | The five Maclaurin series to memorise
- eˣ = 1 + x + x²/2! + x³/3! + …
- sin x = x − x³/3! + x⁵/5! − …
- cos x = 1 − x²/2! + x⁴/4! − …
- ln(1 + x) = x − x²/2 + x³/3 − … ;  1/(1 − x) = 1 + x + x² + …
> Substitution builds new ones instantly

quiz 3 | Maclaurin series for e^(−x²)?
* 1 − x² + x⁴/2! − x⁶/3! + …
o 1 − x + x²/2! − …
o 1 + x² + x⁴/2! + …
o −x² + x⁴ − x⁶ + …
= Substitute −x² into eˣ.
= Integrate it term by term and you can compute the error function by hand.
> Series turn impossible limits into easy ones

quiz 4 | lim x→0 (sin x − x)/x³ = ?
* −1/6
o 0
o 1/6
o −1/3
= sin x − x = −x³/6 + x⁵/120 − … Divide by x³ → −1/6.
> Coefficients can be read straight off the series

quiz 4 | f(x) = x·e^(x²). What is f⁽⁵⁾(0)?
* 60
o 1/2
o 120
o 5
= x·e^(x²) = x + x³ + x⁵/2 + … so the x⁵ coefficient is 1/2.
= f⁽⁵⁾(0)/5! = 1/2, so f⁽⁵⁾(0) = 60.
> No differentiating five times required

real 4 | Einstein's E = mc² hides in a Taylor series
- Relativistic energy is E = γmc², with γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²).
- Expand for small v: γ ≈ 1 + v²/(2c²) + …
- So E ≈ mc² + ½mv² + …: *rest energy plus the kinetic energy from school physics.*
> Next: how wrong is a truncated series?

## c2-rem | Taylor error | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-tay
explain 3 | The Lagrange remainder bounds your error
- Stop after the degree-n term. The error is f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(c)(x − a)ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)! for some c between a and x.
- Bound the (n + 1)th derivative and you bound the error.
? Lagrange remainder for a degree-n Taylor polynomial? :: f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(c)(x − a)ⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1)! for some c between a and x
> How many terms for e to 6 decimals?

quiz 4 | How many terms of 1 + 1 + 1/2! + 1/3! + … give e to within 10⁻⁶?
* About 10
o About 100
o About 3
o About 1,000
= The error after the 1/n! term is less than 3/(n + 1)!.
= 3/10! ≈ 8 × 10⁻⁷. Factorials crush errors fast.
> Alternating series give an even simpler bound

quiz 3 | Approximating sin(0.1) by 0.1, the error is at most…
* (0.1)³/6 ≈ 0.00017
o 0.1
o 0.01
o 0.001
= Alternating series: error ≤ first omitted term, x³/3!.
> Calculators don't use raw Taylor series though

story 4 | How calculators actually compute sin x
- Many early calculators used CORDIC: rotate a vector by a sequence of fixed angles using only shifts and adds.
- Modern chips use range reduction plus short, carefully optimised polynomials.
- *The polynomials aren't Taylor series.* They minimise the worst-case error instead (minimax).
> Next: curves defined by a moving point

## c2-param | Parametric curves | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-rem
explain 3 | Parametric curves describe motion
- x = f(t), y = g(t): a point moving over time.
- Slope: dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt).
- x = cos t, y = sin t traces a circle. x = t − sin t, y = 1 − cos t traces a cycloid.
> The cycloid answers two famous questions

story 5! | The brachistochrone: the fastest slide is not a straight line
- 1696: Johann Bernoulli asked for the curve along which a bead slides fastest between two points.
- The answer is a cycloid. Newton solved it overnight and sent it in anonymously.
- Bernoulli recognised the author anyway: *"the lion by his claw".*
> The cycloid is also the tautochrone: every start point arrives at the bottom at the same time

quiz 3 | x = t², y = t³. What is dy/dx at t = 2?
* 3
o 12
o 4
o 3/4
= dy/dt = 3t² = 12, dx/dt = 2t = 4. Ratio 3.
> Arc length for parametric curves

quiz 3 | Arc length of x = cos t, y = sin t for t from 0 to 2π?
* 2π
o π
o 4π
o 1
= √(sin²t + cos²t) = 1. Integrate 1 over 2π.
> Next: polar coordinates

## c2-polar | Polar coordinates | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-param
explain 2 | Polar coordinates: distance and angle
- x = r cos θ, y = r sin θ. r² = x² + y².
- r = 2 is a circle. θ = π/4 is a line. r = θ is a spiral.
- Roses: r = cos(kθ) has k petals if k is odd, 2k if k is even.
> Area in polar uses wedges, not rectangles

explain 3 | Polar area
- A thin wedge has area ½r²dθ.
- $ A = ½ ∫ r(θ)² dθ
- The cardioid r = 1 + cos θ has area 3π/2.
> Test yourself

quiz 3 | Area enclosed by r = 2cos θ?
* π
o 2π
o 4π
o π/2
= It's a circle of radius 1. Or: ½∫₋π/2^π/2 4cos²θ dθ = π.
> Next: differential equations

## c2-ode1 | First-order ODEs | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-polar
explain 3 | Separable equations: split and integrate
- dy/dx = g(x)h(y). Rearrange: dy/h(y) = g(x)dx. Integrate both sides.
- dy/dx = xy: ln|y| = x²/2 + C, so y = Ae^(x²/2).
> Newton's law of cooling is separable

quiz 3 | Coffee at 90°C cools in a 20°C room: T′ = −k(T − 20). Solution?
* T = 20 + 70e^(−kt)
o T = 90e^(−kt)
o T = 20 + 90e^(−kt)
o T = 70 − 20e^(−kt)
= T − 20 decays exponentially from 70.
> Detectives use this to estimate time of death

real 3 | Time of death from cooling
- A body cools roughly according to Newton's law of cooling.
- Two temperature readings an hour apart give k. Then solve back to 37°C.
- *Forensic estimates add corrections for clothing, air flow and the first hours of plateau.*
> Linear first-order equations need an integrating factor

explain 4 | The integrating factor
- y′ + P(x)y = Q(x). Multiply by μ = e^(∫P dx).
- The left side becomes (μy)′. Integrate both sides.
- y′ + y = eˣ: μ = eˣ, (eˣy)′ = e²ˣ, y = eˣ/2 + Ce⁻ˣ.
? Integrating factor for y′ + P(x)y = Q(x)? :: μ = e^(∫P dx)
> Logistic growth: when exponentials hit a ceiling

explain 3 | The logistic equation
- $ dP/dt = rP(1 − P/K)
- Small P: exponential growth. Near the carrying capacity K: growth stops.
- Solution is an S-curve. Epidemics, product adoption and yeast in a jar all follow it roughly.
> Some differential equations are chaotic

story 4 | Lorenz found chaos by rounding a number
- 1961: Edward Lorenz restarted a weather simulation, typing 0.506 instead of 0.506127.
- The forecast diverged completely within simulated weeks.
- *The butterfly effect.* His three simple ODEs became the most famous chaotic system.
> Next: second-order equations

## c2-ode2 | Second-order linear ODEs | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-ode1
explain 3 | Constant-coefficient ODEs: guess eʳˣ
- ay″ + by′ + cy = 0. Try y = eʳˣ: ar² + br + c = 0.
- Two real roots: two exponentials. Repeated root: eʳˣ and xeʳˣ.
- Complex roots α ± iβ: e^(αx)(A cos βx + B sin βx). *Oscillation.*
? What does a complex root of the characteristic equation mean physically? :: Oscillation (sines and cosines), damped or growing by e^(αx)
> The spring is the most important ODE in physics

quiz 3 | y″ + 9y = 0. General solution?
* A cos 3x + B sin 3x
o Ae³ˣ + Be⁻³ˣ
o A cos 9x + B sin 9x
o (A + Bx)e³ˣ
= r² + 9 = 0, r = ±3i.
= Simple harmonic motion with angular frequency 3.
> Add friction and the oscillation dies away

predict 4 | A damped spring: y″ + 2y′ + y = 0. Does it oscillate?
* No, it's critically damped
o Yes, slowly decaying
o Yes, growing
= r² + 2r + 1 = (r + 1)² = 0: a repeated root.
= Critical damping returns fastest without overshooting. Car suspension aims near it.
> Push it at the right frequency and you get resonance

explain 4 | Resonance
- y″ + ω₀²y = cos(ωt). If ω = ω₀, the solution contains t·sin(ω₀t): it grows without bound.
- Real systems have damping, so the amplitude peaks instead of exploding.
- *Opera singers shattering glass, swings going higher, MRI machines: all resonance.*
> Tacoma Narrows wasn't simple resonance, though

myth 4 F | The Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapsed in 1940 because wind matched its resonant frequency.
= The popular textbook story is a simplification.
= The collapse is explained by aeroelastic flutter: the bridge's own motion fed energy from steady wind into a twisting mode.
= Steady 64 km/h wind, no periodic pushing required.
> Next: the Laplace transform

## c2-lap | Laplace transform | maths | 3 | calc2 | c2-ode2
explain 4 | The Laplace transform turns calculus into algebra
- $ F(s) = ∫₀^∞ f(t)e^(−st) dt
- Derivatives become multiplication: L{f′} = sF(s) − f(0).
- Solve the ODE as an algebra problem in s, then transform back.
? Laplace transform of f′? :: sF(s) − f(0)
> A table of transforms does most of the work

quiz 3 | L{e^(at)} = ?
* 1/(s − a)
o 1/(s + a)
o a/s
o e^(as)
= ∫₀^∞ e^((a − s)t) dt = 1/(s − a), for s > a.
> Engineers live in the s-domain

real 4 | Control engineers think in Laplace space
- Cruise control, drones and thermostats are designed as transfer functions G(s).
- Stability means every pole of G(s) has negative real part.
- *A pole in the right half-plane means the system blows up.*
> Next: Fourier series in practice

## c2-fourp | Fourier series practice | maths | 2 | calc2 | c2-lap
explain 3 | Finding Fourier coefficients
- f(x) = a₀/2 + Σ (aₙ cos nx + bₙ sin nx) on [−π, π].
- $ aₙ = (1/π)∫ f(x)cos(nx) dx,  bₙ = (1/π)∫ f(x)sin(nx) dx
- Works because sines and cosines of different frequencies are *orthogonal*: they integrate to zero against each other.
> Symmetry halves the work

quiz 3 | f(x) = x on [−π, π]. Which coefficients are zero?
* All the aₙ (cosine terms)
o All the bₙ (sine terms)
o None
o All of them
= x is odd. Odd functions only need sines.
> Fourier series prove the Basel result too

story 4 | Parseval's theorem: energy is the same in both pictures
- The integral of f² equals the sum of the squares of the coefficients (suitably scaled).
- Apply it to f(x) = x and out drops Σ1/n² = π²/6 again.
- *Energy in time = energy in frequency.*
> Next: the Fourier transform

## c2-ft | Fourier transform | maths | 3 | calc2 | c2-fourp
explain 4 | The Fourier transform: a spectrum for any signal
- $ f̂(ω) = ∫ f(t)e^(−iωt) dt
- It says how much of each frequency ω is in the signal.
- Non-periodic signals get a continuous spectrum instead of discrete harmonics.
> A narrow pulse has a wide spectrum

predict 4 | Squeeze a pulse to half the duration. What happens to its spectrum?
* It becomes twice as wide
o It becomes half as wide
o It stays the same
= Time and frequency widths trade off: Δt·Δω ≥ a constant.
= In quantum mechanics this same fact is the uncertainty principle.
> The FFT made the transform practical

story 5 | The FFT: the algorithm that runs the modern world
- 1965: Cooley and Tukey publish the Fast Fourier Transform: n log n operations instead of n².
- Gauss had found essentially the same method around 1805 while tracking asteroids. Unpublished.
- *Audio, Wi-Fi, 4G, image compression and MRI all depend on it.*
> Convolution becomes multiplication under the transform

explain 4 | The convolution theorem
- Convolution (f * g) smears one function by another: blurs, echoes, filters.
- $ (f * g)^ = f̂ · ĝ
- So filtering a song is just multiplying its spectrum. FFT, multiply, inverse FFT.
> Next: complex numbers properly

## c2-cplx | Complex numbers | maths | 1 | calc2 | c2-ft
hook 4 | i was invented to solve cubics, not x² = −1
- 1500s: Cardano's formula for cubics sometimes needed √(negative numbers) along the way, even when all three answers were real.
- Bombelli worked through the arithmetic and the imaginary parts cancelled to give real roots.
- *Mathematicians were forced to accept them.*
> Multiply by i and you rotate by 90°

explain 3 | Complex multiplication is rotation and scaling
- Write z = re^(iθ). Multiply two: lengths multiply, angles add.
- Multiplying by i rotates by 90°. i² = −1 is two quarter-turns: a half-turn.
- *That's why complex numbers model waves and AC circuits so well.*
? What does multiplying by i do geometrically? :: Rotates by 90° anticlockwise
> De Moivre gives every trig identity for free

quiz 3 | (cos θ + i sin θ)³ = ?
* cos 3θ + i sin 3θ
o cos³θ + i sin³θ
o 3cos θ + 3i sin θ
o cos 3θ − i sin 3θ
= De Moivre: (e^(iθ))³ = e^(3iθ).
= Expand the left side and match real parts: cos 3θ = 4cos³θ − 3cos θ.
> Roots of unity are evenly spaced around a circle

quiz 3 | How many complex solutions does z⁶ = 1 have?
* 6
o 2
o 1
o Infinitely many
= e^(2πik/6) for k = 0,…,5: the vertices of a regular hexagon.
= Fundamental Theorem of Algebra: a degree-n polynomial has exactly n complex roots (with multiplicity).
> Calculus 2 done. Calculus 3: more dimensions
